179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (Across the East and Horn of Africa, one of the most consistent findings across this study's key informant interviews is that pastoralist women are already functioning as peacebuilders, mediating disputes, de-escalating tensions, and maintaining social cohesion across ethnic and clan lines. Yet this contribution happens almost entirely outside the formal institutions that govern rangelands, water, and peace. Key to note is that elders' councils, natural-resource governance bodies, and peace committees remain predominantly male, and women's mediation work is rarely recognized, resourced or given decision-making authority.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (This brief argues that strengthening women's formal participation in rangeland governance and peacebuilding is not simply a matter of equity but is a practical necessity for durable peace and sustainable resource management. Evidence from Ethiopia's participatory rangeland management programmes and Kenya's women's peace networks shows that when women are formally included in governance and mediation structures, communities report improved trust, more equitable resource access, and more resilient conflict resolution. The evidence gaps this study identifies is not whether women can contribute but is it is about whether institutions will let them do so on an equal basis with their male counterparts.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
