192 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 103 0 R
/T (Rangeland governance and peacebuilding are not separate policy domains in pastoral contexts; they are functionally the same terrain. Disputes over water points, grazing corridors and migration routes are simultaneously natural resource management challenges, as well as peace and security challenges. Decisions about who controls access to a borehole or a grazing corridor are, in practice, decisions about who has power to prevent or provoke conflict. This means that excluding women from rangeland governance is not a narrow gap in one sector; it is a structural gap in the region's entire conflict-prevention architecture.)
/E (Rangeland governance and peacebuilding are not separate policy domains in pastoral contexts; they are functionally the same terrain. Disputes over water points, grazing corridors and migration routes are simultaneously natural resource management challenges, as well as peace and security challenges. Decisions about who controls access to a borehole or a grazing corridor are, in practice, decisions about who has power to prevent or provoke conflict. This means that excluding women from rangeland governance is not a narrow gap in one sector; it is a structural gap in the region's entire conflict-prevention architecture.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
193 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 103 0 R
/T (This brief builds on evidence generated specifically for this study emanating from key informant interviews with 20 pastoralist women-led, humanitarian, climate-resilience, policy and peacebuilding organizations across Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania, and South Africa. That evidence shows women actively working to fill this gap informally, assuming different roles as mediators, counsellors, community mobilizers, and protectors of social cohesion but without the institutional standing that would let their work scale, be sustained, or influence formal decisions about resource allocation and conflict resolution.)
/E (This brief builds on evidence generated specifically for this study emanating from key informant interviews with 20 pastoralist women-led, humanitarian, climate-resilience, policy and peacebuilding organizations across Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania, and South Africa. That evidence shows women actively working to fill this gap informally, assuming different roles as mediators, counsellors, community mobilizers, and protectors of social cohesion but without the institutional standing that would let their work scale, be sustained, or influence formal decisions about resource allocation and conflict resolution.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
