219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (The study findings align closely with a substantial and growing evidence base on women's roles in natural-resource governance and peacebuilding across Africa. UN Women's work with Kenya's peace committees provides some of the most direct corroboration available. For instance, in four counties namely Marsabit, Kitui, Kwale and Turkana, supported engagement with local peace and security processes contributed to an increase in women's participation in local peace committees from 29 percent in 2019 to 34 percent in 2023 \(UN Women Africa, 2023\). This is precisely the kind of incremental, institutionally supported gain in formal participation that this brief's recommendations are designed to accelerate and replicate.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (Perhaps the most directly relevant peer-reviewed evidence comes from Ethiopia's participatory rangeland management \(PRM\) programme. A review of PRM implementation across the Oromia and Afar regions found that women participated in the PRM process at rates broadly comparable to men, and that community members widely believed the programme had improved women's roles in rangeland governance leadership, decision-making and access to rangeland resources describing this as part of a broader transformative process toward gender equality in pastoralist societies \(Flintan & Eba, 2023\). This finding directly supports this brief's core argument: formal inclusion in governance structures produces measurable gains, not merely symbolic ones.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (Comparative research reinforces this pattern beyond Ethiopia. A comparative case study of women's grassroots environmental governance in post-conflict Liberia and climate-stressed Kenya found that women's participation in environmental governance is closely linked to peacebuilding outcomes, even as systemic legal, sociocultural and institutional barriers continue to constrain their influence \(Shulika & Shafiei, 2026\). Similarly, community-based degraded land rehabilitation programmes reviewed across multiple African contexts were found to improve women's roles in leadership, decision-making and access to and control over resources, with downstream effects on gender equality and women's empowerment in rural communities more broadly \(Flintan & Eba, 2023\).)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
