227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 106 0 R
/T (At the same time, the global data underscore how far formal peace architecture still has to go. A twenty-year review of UN Security Council Resolution 1325 implementation found that women were signatories to only 13 of 130 peace agreements signed between 1990 and 2014, with no meaningful increase in that share over time, and that by 2019 women still made up under 5 percent of military personnel and under 11 percent of police personnel in UN peacekeeping missions \(Tripp, 2020\). This global pattern of formal exclusion despite informal contribution is the same structural gap this brief identifies in pastoral rangeland and peace governance specifically.)
/E (At the same time, the global data underscore how far formal peace architecture still has to go. A twenty-year review of UN Security Council Resolution 1325 implementation found that women were signatories to only 13 of 130 peace agreements signed between 1990 and 2014, with no meaningful increase in that share over time, and that by 2019 women still made up under 5 percent of military personnel and under 11 percent of police personnel in UN peacekeeping missions \(Tripp, 2020\). This global pattern of formal exclusion despite informal contribution is the same structural gap this brief identifies in pastoral rangeland and peace governance specifically.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 106 0 R
/T (Taken together, this literature confirms our findings that women's informal contribution to peacebuilding and resource governance is extensive and well documented across the continent, and that formal institutional inclusion where it has been deliberately built, as in Ethiopia's PRM programme or Kenya's supported peace committees produces measurable governance and peace benefits.)
/E (Taken together, this literature confirms our findings that women's informal contribution to peacebuilding and resource governance is extensive and well documented across the continent, and that formal institutional inclusion where it has been deliberately built, as in Ethiopia's PRM programme or Kenya's supported peace committees produces measurable governance and peace benefits.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
