248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (Despite women's active informal peacebuilding role, formal peace committees, elders' councils and natural-resource governance bodies across the study countries remain predominantly male. Respondents identified patriarchal norms, low literacy, financial dependence, limited mobility, unpaid care work, intimidation and exclusion from customary structures as the primary constraints on women's formal participation.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (UN Women's tracking of women's representation on Kenyan peace committees provides a useful, if sobering, benchmark and demonstrates that even with sustained donor and government support across four counties, women's participation rose only from 29 percent to 34 percent between 2019 and 2023 \(UN Women Africa, 2023\). Though this is meaningful progress, it still falls short of parity, and achieved only through deliberate, resourced intervention rather than as a natural byproduct of programming.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 108 0 R
/T (3. Land and resource rights are a structural precondition for participation)
/E (3. Land and resource rights are a structural precondition for participation)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (Land rights emerged as an especially significant barrier in Tanzania and Kenya, where cultural and institutional norms continue to limit women's ownership of land, livestock and other productive assets, even where women carry the primary burden of securing food, water and income for their households. In Somalia, the dominant pattern was noted to be different because exclusion is rooted more in clan-based leadership structures and formal institutional arrangements than in land tenure specifically.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
