/P 109 0 R
/T (4. Formal inclusion produces measurable governance and peace benefits)
/E (4. Formal inclusion produces measurable governance and peace benefits)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (Where this study's respondents described positive examples of women's formal inclusion in governance, the effects mirrored what the wider comparative literature documents notably greater trust, more equitable resource access and stronger conflict-resolution outcomes. This is not a normative claim about fairness alone but an empirical claim about what makes rangeland governance and peace processes work better.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (The Ethiopian PRM review found that community members across Oromia and Afar broadly believed the programme had improved women's roles in rangeland leadership and decision-making and had encouraged a wider transformative process of gender equality in pastoralist governance \(Flintan & Eba, 2023\). Complementary evidence from Isiolo, Kenya, found that integrating climate adaptation into peacebuilding through community-based water conservation and rangeland rehabilitation involving women enhanced drought resilience while also reducing inter-clan conflict over resources \(Medina et al., 2025\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
261 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 109 0 R
/T (5. Meaningful participation requires more than a seat at the table)
/E (5. Meaningful participation requires more than a seat at the table)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
262 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (Respondents were clear that genuine participation requires enforceable rights, voice, information, finance and authority and not simply an invitation to attend meetings that continue to be dominated by existing power structures. This distinction between token representation \(tokenism\) and substantive authority recurs throughout the global literature on women, peace and security.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
263 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (A twenty-year review of UNSCR 1325 implementation found that women remain grossly underrepresented in formal peace processes globally, they were signatories to only 13 of 130 peace agreements signed between 1990 and 2014, and their peacebuilding activities, even when substantial are frequently ignored by formal processes, even as those same activities build bridges across ethnic, religious and political divisions \(Tripp, 2020\). This global pattern reinforces the finding that the barrier facing pastoralist women is not a lack of will or activity on their part, but rather a lack of formal recognition and authority.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
