<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (The recommendations below are derived directly from the key informant interview findings presented in this brief and are further supported by the regional and global literature reviewed above. They translate this brief's core argument that formal governance architecture has not caught up with women's already-extensive peacebuilding and resource-stewardship work into specific institutional actions, naming the mechanism, the responsible actors, and an existing model to replicate wherever one exists.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 111 0 R
/T (1. Formal Governance Representation)
/E (1. Formal Governance Representation)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 617 0 R
/E (Formalize seats and voting authority for women in rangeland and peace governance - guarantee women's representation, with voting rights rather than observer status, on peace committees, elders' councils and natural-resource governance bodies, following Kenya's supported peace-committee model.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 618 0 R
/E (Adapt Ethiopia's Participatory Rangeland Management model directly - pilot and scale governance models that explicitly build women's participation into rangeland decision-making processes from the outset, rather than assuming inclusion will follow generic community engagement \(Flintan & Eba, 2023\).)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 619 0 R
/E (Move beyond token representation - pair any formal seat for women with the information, finance and enforceable rights needed to exercise real decision-making authority, since participation without authority replicates exclusion in a new form.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
