1221 0 obj
<<
/Title (Formal peace committees, elders' councils, and natural-resource governance bodies remain predominantly male across the study countries.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1215 0 R
/Prev 1217 0 R
/Next 1226 0 R
>>
endobj
1222 0 obj
<<
/Title (Where women have gained formal roles in resource governance, respondents and comparative evidence link this to improved trust, more equitable access and stronger conflict-resolution outcomes.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1215 0 R
/Prev 1227 0 R
/Next 1218 0 R
>>
endobj
1223 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1219 0 R
/Next 1228 0 R
>>
endobj
1224 0 obj
<<
/Title (Taken together, this literature confirms our findings that women's informal contribution to peacebuilding and resource governance is extensive and well documented across the continent, and that formal institutional inclusion where it has been deliberately built, as in Ethiopia's PRM programme or Kenya's supported peace committees produces measurable governance and peace benefits.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1228 0 R
/Next 1220 0 R
>>
endobj
1225 0 obj
<<
/Title (1. Women are already peacebuilders albeit informally)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1220 0 R
/First 1229 0 R
/Last 1230 0 R
/Count -3
>>
endobj
1226 0 obj
<<
/Title (Barriers to women's formal participation include patriarchal norms, low literacy, financial dependence, limited mobility, unpaid care burdens and exclusion from customary structures.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1215 0 R
/Prev 1221 0 R
/Next 1227 0 R
>>
endobj
1227 0 obj
<<
/Title (Land and resource rights are a particularly acute barrier in Kenya and Tanzania; in Somalia, exclusion from clan-based leadership and formal institutions is the dominant pattern.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1215 0 R
/Prev 1226 0 R
/Next 1222 0 R
>>
endobj
1228 0 obj
<<
/Title (At the same time, the global data underscore how far formal peace architecture still has to go. A twenty-year review of UN Security Council Resolution 1325 implementation found that women were signatories to only 13 of 130 peace agreements signed between 1990 and 2014, with no meaningful increase in that share over time, and that by 2019 women still made up under 5 percent of military personnel and under 11 percent of police personnel in UN peacekeeping missions \(Tripp, 2020\). This global pattern of formal exclusion despite informal contribution is the same structural gap this brief identifies in pastoral rangeland and peace governance specifically.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1223 0 R
/Next 1224 0 R
>>
endobj
1229 0 obj
<<
/Title (Across the dataset, women were consistently described as mediators, counsellors, mobilizers and protectors of social cohesion. Their social relationships and responsibility for family welfare often position them as first responders when tensions emerge as they engage in family-level negotiation, reconciliation, peace caravans and community dialogue well before formal institutions become involved.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1225 0 R
/Next 1231 0 R
>>
endobj
1230 0 obj
<<
/Title (This pattern was reported consistently by key informants across all four study countries i.e. Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania and South Africa, thus indicating a shared regional dynamic rather than a localized effect. This finding is strongly corroborated by UN Women's documentation of Kenyan women peacebuilders such as Mary Mariach and Christine Lemuya, who mediate longstanding inter-tribal disputes in Kenya's ASAL regions through local dialogue and outreach, using approaches that, per UN Women's reporting, male-dominated leadership structures have not always been willing to take \(UN Women, 2022\).)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1225 0 R
/Prev 1231 0 R
>>
endobj
1231 0 obj
